<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liaquat Ali Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liaquat Ali Khan was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a> and close associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> who became the first prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947. He led the new state through partition, the refugee crisis and the first war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, and moved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/objectives-resolution">Objectives Resolution</a> of 1949 on the constitution. He was assassinated in Rawalpindi in October 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liaquat-ali-khan.jpg" alt="Liaquat Ali Khan" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author - but due to age, image in public domain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liaquat_Ali_Khan_1945.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Liaquat Ali Khan was born on 1 October 1895 in Karnal, in Punjab Province, the second of four sons of a wealthy landowner of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> family. His grandfather, Nawab Ahmad Ali, had supported the British during the uprising of 1857–1858 and was rewarded with honours and the remission of his rent. Liaquat was taught at home and in Karnal, instructed in the Qur'an and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, and later studied at Aligarh Muslim University and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. His biographer Muhammad Reza Kazimi notes that little is known of his early life, which has to be pieced together from mostly hagiographic writings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1923 and entered national politics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> invited him to join, but after meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> he refused and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a>. He was elected to the legislative council of the United Provinces in 1926, became its deputy president in 1932 and in 1940 entered the Central Legislative Assembly. In 1933 he and his wife Begum Ra'ana met Jinnah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and urged him to return to India. On 12 April 1936 Liaquat was named honorary general secretary of the League, an office he held until 1947.</p><p>After independence in 1947 he became the first prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and also held the posts of foreign minister, defence minister and minister for frontier regions. His government faced the first war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, rivalry from socialist and communist groups, and a growing dependence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> aid. On 12 March 1949 the Constituent Assembly passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/objectives-resolution">Objectives Resolution</a>, which he called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a> of Pakistan's constitutional history; the Law Minister, Jogendra Nath Mandal, argued against it. In 1950 he signed the Liaquat–Nehru Pact with Nehru to ease tension and protect religious minorities on both sides of the border. After Jinnah's death he did not take the office of governor-general, which went to Khawaja Nazimuddin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 16 October 1951 Liaquat was shot twice in the chest while addressing a gathering of 100,000 at the Company Bagh in Rawalpindi. Police shot the presumed killer, Said Akbar, an Afghan national, at once. Liaquat died after reaching hospital, and the motive has never been fully revealed. He was buried at the Mazar-e-Quaid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, and the park was renamed Liaquat Bagh. He is known as Quaid-e-Millat, Leader of the Nation, and Shaheed-e-Millat, Martyr of the Nation. His assassination was the first political murder of a civilian leader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and remains unsolved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liaquat Ali Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jinnah became the first governor-general and died of tuberculosis on 11 September 1948; <b>Liaquat Ali Khan</b> was the first prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Jinnah died in 1948 and prime minister <b>Liaquat Ali Khan</b> was assassinated in 1951.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Karnal, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.69,76.99&amp;year=1931" aria-label="Karnal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Liaquat Ali Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4042 4972h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4042 4972h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.69,76.99&amp;year=1931">What was here in 1931? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
